    <div class="post-container" id="fpc-2790806">
                      
		<div class="post" id="post-2790806">
		<div class="long">
			<div class="head">
									<div class="options">
						<a href="javascript:;" onclick="togglePostFold(event,2790806)" class="btn btn-default btn-small btn-sm">Fold</a>
					</div>
								<div class="title" id="post-title-2790806">
																		
															</div>
				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser deleted" data-id="2303803"><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/common--images/avatars/default/a16.png" alt=""/>(account deleted)</span> <span class="odate time_1492057430 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">13 Apr 2017 04:23</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2790806">
									

<p>I'm glad you picked up on something in it beyond it being just another drama piece (which it easily is one in many lights). This was pretty much vent writing for me, and my increasingly bad dysphoria played a huge part in how the narrative worked out, from the character himself to the transphobic security director and all other aspects.</p>
<p>I do feel the need to point out something, though, just in general, because someone talked to me in IRC about this briefly: <em>Trans people are not always or by default miserable</em> (even though anyone with basic sociopolitical knowledge knows that we face hardships the majority of people don't). Fiction doesn't represent us well sometimes, and I can say even as the author of this piece that this is perhaps an example of another addition to too much fiction in which the trans characters are unhappy or treated badly. I don't recommend anyone interpret this as a by-the-book representation of how trans people struggle, even putting the fictional aspect aside. This tale is me personally channeling negative feelings I've been having lately into fiction and getting lucky that something coherent came out of the end product; it's not an intentionally thought-out representation of anyone, and it's not even an intentionally thought-out narrative.</p>
<p>Pardon any wonky sentences or bad phrasing in the above paragraphs, as I'm not quite functioning at full capacity right now, but felt the need to make this reply.</p>

							</div>

								
					
